Make native 60 FPS survive a savestate load.

Reported in play as "the fallback still works, it drops to 30", with the
player's own diagnosis - "maybe quick load keeps the door closed" - which was
right. The run log shows it: `savestate: LOADED slot 0` about eight seconds
after boot, before the main loop had run once; after it the heartbeat read
gate_open 0 and cpu_now 100 for the rest of the session, backoffs 0.

The patched words live in guest RAM and so travel inside a savestate. This
module's own state - gate open, clock raised, VSync widened - is host state
and does not. A slot saved at 60 holds the OPEN gate word. Loaded into a
session that had not opened the gate yet, RAM said open while the module said
closed, and the open path accepted only ORIG: it treated our own patch as a
foreign revision and never opened. The dirty-text check then correctly ran the
restored OPEN gate, so the game DID attempt 60 - at the 100% clock, where busy
frames need two fields. Hence 30, with the mode reporting itself on and no
back-off recorded.

Fix:
  - The open path accepts OPEN as well as ORIG and adopts it: re-marks the
    word as patched code, raises the clock, widens VSync.
  - interrupts_resync_after_restore(), which every restore reaches (quick
    load, rewind, netplay rollback, self-check), now flags the module via
    crash2_60fps_note_restore(); the next outer loop reconciles.
  - With the mode OFF, a restored 60-era state has its original words put
    back. Before, it ran the game half-60 at the stock clock. This only
    happens straight after a restore so the debug server's frame-gate probes,
    which write the same word on purpose, are not fought.
  - Demo suspension closes the gate by the RAM word as well as by the flag,
    so a state loaded into an attract loop cannot keep a stray OPEN gate.
  - One stderr line when a restore leaves RAM and module disagreeing - silent
    otherwise, since a held rewind restores many times a second.

Neither file is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake. The pre-existing bug predates
0033; this fixes it on top of 0033.

